#Why RPF needed in PIM ?, When the PIM Tree with *PIM states/signalling* is Built to be loop free already 

PIM Tree or the PIM Forwarding states are in *Control Plane*.

In *Data Plane* While the Actual data packets still needs a check while forwarding.

The PIM tree defines downstream interfaces (OIL — Outgoing Interface List). But a router can receive the same multicast stream on multiple interfaces — especially during:
	• SPT switchover (from RPT to Shortest Path Tree)
	• Topology changes / reconvergence
	• Redundant links
  Without RPF, the router has no way to know which incoming copy is legitimate
	•To AVoid Transient Loops During Convergence
   Even if the steady-state tree is loop-free, during link failures or routing reconvergence, packets can temporarily arrive on unexpected interfaces. 
    RPF acts as a real-time sanity check on every packet, not just at tree-build time.
 •Misdelivered Traffic
  Without RPF, a multicast packet injected or reflected from the wrong part of the network could be forwarded endlessly — RPF is the data-plane gate.
Think of the PIM tree as a road map — it tells you which roads exist. RPF is the checkpoint at each intersection that verifies traffic is actually coming from the right direction, not just that the road exists. A correct map doesn't eliminate the need for directional validation at runtime.

Summary
The PIM tree being loop-free is a topological guarantee (control plane). RPF is a per-packet forwarding correctness check (data plane). 
They solve two different problems — one builds the right structure, the other ensures only legitimate traffic flows through it.
PIM Tree   →   Builds the correct highway system
RPF Check  →   Traffic police at every Junction


##During RPT switchover we get any extra packets to the Receiver ?
 
Yes — the receiver CAN get duplicate packets during SPT switchover. They are not always dropped by intermediate routers.

What Actually Happens During SPT Switchover
Phase 1: Traffic flowing via RPT (through RP)
         Source → RP → DR → Receiver

Phase 2: SPT Join sent upstream toward Source
         Source → DR → Receiver  (new shorter path)

Overlap Window: BOTH paths are briefly active
During this overlap window, the Last Hop Router (LHR) receives the same multicast stream on two interfaces simultaneously — one from RPT, one from SPT.

What the LHR Does
Packet arrives on SPT interface (toward Source)
→ RPF check PASSES  ✅
→ Forwarded to receiver

Packet arrives on RPT interface (toward RP)
→ RPF check FAILS  ❌  (RPF now points toward Source, not RP)
→ DROPPED by LHR
So the RPT copy gets dropped once the RPF interface flips to the SPT path.

But Here's the Catch — The Transition Moment
Timeline:
|----RPT only----|--BOTH active--|----SPT only----|
                 ↑               ↑
            SPT join sent    RPF flips to SPT
            
         During this gap → DUPLICATES reach receiver
	• The moment SPT traffic starts arriving but before RPF fully flips — both copies can be forwarded
	• This window is very brief (milliseconds) but duplicates do reach the receiver


The PIM-SM Mechanism That Limits This
Once LHR is fully on SPT, it sends a Prune toward the RP on the RPT:
LHR → (S,G,rpt) Prune → RP
This tears down the RPT branch, eliminating the duplicate path entirely.

Complete Timeline
1. LHR receiving via RPT
2. LHR traffic rate exceeds threshold → triggers SPT Join
3. SPT Join travels upstream toward Source DR
4. Source starts sending on SPT path
5. ← Brief duplicate window here →
6. RPF flips to SPT interface
7. RPT packets now fail RPF → dropped at LHR
8. LHR sends (S,G,rpt) Prune to RP
9. RP tears down that RPT branch
10. Clean SPT forwarding only

Bottom Line
	RPF limits the duplicate window but cannot eliminate it entirely during the transition. A small burst of duplicates reaching the receiver is expected and normal behavior in PIM-SM SPT switchover — it's not a bug, it's an accepted trade-off for the simplicity of the mechanism.






